  <h2>Where the airport is</h2>
  <p>
    Cluj-Napoca's airport, <strong>Avram Iancu Cluj International Airport (CLJ)</strong>, sits
    about 9 km east of the city centre, along Strada Traian Vuia. That's close enough that
    getting into town is quick and cheap by any option below; there's no reason to pay
    "airport transfer" prices here.
  </p>

  <h2>Option 1: Public transport (cheapest)</h2>
  <p>
    According to the <a href="https://airportcluj.ro/en/passengers/transport/" rel="noopener">airport's
    official transport page</a>, three lines connect the airport with the central area, each
    taking roughly <strong>26 minutes</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Line A1E</strong>, the express service</li>
    <li><strong>Line 5</strong>, urban line</li>
    <li><strong>Line 8</strong>, urban line</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The stop is right outside the terminal. Current timetables are on the
    <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/timetables/urban-lines" rel="noopener">CTP Cluj urban
    lines page</a> (CTP is the city's public transport operator).
  </p>

  <h3>Tickets: the catch for fresh arrivals</h3>
  <p>
    Locals often buy tickets by SMS to <strong>7479</strong>: texting the line number buys a
    45-minute single-line ticket (0.72&nbsp;EUR + VAT), and the letter <strong>T</strong> buys a
    one-hour ticket for all lines (1.40&nbsp;EUR + VAT), per
    <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/fares/bilete-prin-sms" rel="noopener">CTP's SMS ticket
    page</a>. The catch: <strong>SMS tickets are charged to a Romanian mobile number</strong>, so
    they generally won't work on the foreign SIM you land with.
  </p>
  <p>
    With a foreign SIM, buy a ticket from a machine or kiosk at the stop, or check
    <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/fares/urban-transportation" rel="noopener">CTP's fares
    pages</a> for the current electronic options. If the machine situation at the stop is
    unclear when you land, it happens to plenty of people in their first hour in Romania. It's
    exactly the kind of thing you can <a href="/cluj/foreigner-support/">ask us about</a> in real time.
  </p>

  <h2>Option 2: Bolt or Uber (easiest)</h2>
  <p>
    Both <strong>Bolt</strong> and <strong>Uber</strong> operate in Cluj, and for a first arrival
    with luggage they're the low-stress choice: fixed price shown in the app, no cash needed, no
    language barrier. Order after you exit the terminal and follow the app's pickup point.
    The ride to the centre is short, and outside surge hours it's inexpensive by Western
    European standards.
  </p>

  <h2>Option 3: Taxi</h2>
  <p>
    Licensed taxis in Cluj are metered and honest fares to the centre are modest. Two rules keep
    it that way: use the <strong>official taxi rank</strong> outside the terminal (the airport
    lists local <a href="https://airportcluj.ro/en/" rel="noopener">taxi companies on its
    site</a>), and skip anyone who approaches you inside the terminal offering a ride, the
    oldest airport trick there is. If in doubt, ask
    the driver for a rough price before getting in, or just use Bolt.
  </p>

  <h2>Arriving late at night</h2>
  <p>
    Cluj does have night service: CTP runs night lines (there's even an SMS ticket code for
    night line 25N), but frequencies are thin and stops may be far from your accommodation.
    After a late landing, the practical answer is Bolt/Uber or a rank taxi.
    Check the <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/timetables/night-lines" rel="noopener">night
    lines timetable</a> if you'd rather ride the bus.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>First-arrival checklist:</strong> screenshot your accommodation address (with the
      Romanian spelling) · have a card that works abroad · keep a little cash in RON for small
      things (ATMs are in the terminal; decline "conversion to your currency," it's a worse
      rate) · install Bolt before you fly · don't rely on SMS tickets until you have a Romanian
      SIM.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Quick questions</h2>
  <h3>Is there a train from the airport?</h3>
  <p>
    No direct rail link from the terminal. Bus, taxi, or ride-hailing are the options that
    matter for arrivals.
  </p>
  <h3>How much cash should I bring?</h3>
  <p>
    Cards are accepted almost everywhere in Cluj. A small amount of RON is handy for the first
    days, but don't exchange large sums at airport rates.
  </p>
  <h3>What if my accommodation falls through on arrival?</h3>
  <p>
    Take a breath, get to the centre, and sort it from a café, and if you want a local to sanity-check
    a last-minute listing before you pay for it, <a href="/cluj/housing-help/">that's literally our
    job</a>.
  </p>
